\ ff-test: lines
\ `is` in a definition, and deferred words whose action goes away.
\
\ `is` wasn't immediate: in a definition it compiled a call that looked
\ for its name only when the definition ran, so a definition couldn't set
\ a deferred word's action as in ANS Forth. And a deferred word whose
\ action was forgotten, or never finished compiling, went on calling the
\ freed word.

: p 32 emit . ;
defer hook
: ten 10 ;
: twenty 20 ;
' ten is hook  hook p cr

\ In a definition the name is read while compiling; the xt is taken from
\ the stack when the definition runs.
: use-twenty ['] twenty is hook ;
: use-any is hook ;
use-twenty hook p cr
' ten use-any hook p cr
see use-twenty

\ The name has to be a deferred word, on the same line.
: bad ['] ten is ten ;
: bad2 is
' ten is
clear

\ A deferred word whose action is forgotten has none left, rather than
\ calling freed memory.
: impl 42 ;
' impl is hook  hook p cr
forget impl
hook

\ Nor one whose action was a definition that failed.
: gone [ ' gone is hook ] 1 zork ;
hook
